 dict(id='MAT-X01',question='Does an intrinsically superior metal explain substitution?',case_ids=['MAT-C02','MAT-C05'],useful_similarities='Both have competing copper-alloy/material traditions and continued older practice; properties depend on processing and task.',important_differences='Canaan compares bronze with iron using archaeological sequences; Andean comparison includes controlled modern arsenical/tin-bronze tests and an imperial distribution interpretation.',evidence_comparability='Mechanical tests and archaeological assemblage frequencies answer different questions; neither supplies comparable prices or household adoption denominators.',connections_nonindependence='The two regional empirical programmes differ, but share scholarly critiques of hardness ranking. Several claims within each case derive from the same paper and must not count as separate replications.',omitted_countercases='Contexts where improved treatment demonstrably precedes task-specific replacement; arsenical/tin-bronze transitions in other regions.',conclusion_limit='Rejects a universal scalar material ranking; does not show performance never causes substitution.'),
 dict(id='MAT-X02',question='Does sustained ironmaking imply fuel depletion and inevitable mineral-fuel adoption?',case_ids=['MAT-C03','MAT-C04'],useful_similarities='Ironmaking requires substantial, suitable fuel supplies and coordinated operation; both retain uncertainty about production intensity.',important_differences='Song–Yuan evidence includes mineral fuel and documentary information; Meroe/Hamadab evidence centres on selected charcoal and modern replicas. Ecology, chronology and product mix differ.',evidence_comparability='Text/chemical fuel evidence and anthracology measure different things. Neither gives matched local forest inventories, prices and output per year.',connections_nonindependence='Meroe experimental and charcoal papers share project chronology. Wagner supplies both technical and output critiques for the Chinese subepisode; no direct historical link is claimed.',omitted_countercases='Measured charcoal woodland management elsewhere; iron regions with accessible coal that continued charcoal; periods of demonstrated fuel shortage.',conclusion_limit='Resource substitution and sustainability are contingent; continued charcoal is a meaningful countercase to inevitability, not proof of no damage.'),
